---
import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import Waterfall from '@/components/Waterfall.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import CodeSwitcher from '@/components/CodeSwitcher.astro'

/* Written here rather than read from an example program: these are queries a
   reader runs against their own daemon, not code CI can execute. */
const breakdown = [
  {
    lang: 'curl' as const,
    label: 'cURL',
    shiki: 'bash',
    code: `# Where the money went last week, by model.
curl -s --get http://localhost:4317/v1/api/analytics/cost \\
  --data-urlencode 'from=-7d' \\
  --data-urlencode 'group_by=model'

# The same week as a daily series, so you can see the day it changed.
curl -s --get http://localhost:4317/v1/api/analytics/cost \\
  --data-urlencode 'from=-7d' \\
  --data-urlencode 'group_by=model' \\
  --data-urlencode 'bucket=1d'
`,
  },
  {
    lang: 'typescript' as const,
    label: 'TypeScript',
    shiki: 'typescript',
    code: `import { Mcpd } from '@mcpd/sdk'

const mcpd = new Mcpd()

const spend = await mcpd.analyticsCost({
  from: '-7d',
  to: '-0s',
  groupBy: 'model',
  bucket: '1d',
})

console.log(\`\${spend.currency} \${spend.totalCost.toFixed(4)} over the window\`)
for (const group of spend.groups) {
  console.log(group.key, group.totalCost, group.inputTokens, group.outputTokens)
}`,
  },
  {
    lang: 'python' as const,
    label: 'Python',
    shiki: 'python',
    code: `from mcpd import Mcpd

mcpd = Mcpd()

spend = mcpd.analytics_cost("-7d", "-0s", group_by="model", bucket="1d")

print(f"{spend.currency} {spend.total_cost:.4f} over the window")
for group in spend.groups:
    print(group.key, group.total_cost, group.input_tokens, group.output_tokens)`,
  },
]

const response = [
  {
    lang: 'curl' as const,
    label: 'CostBreakdown',
    shiki: 'json',
    code: `{
  "from": "2026-08-20T09:00:00Z",
  "to": "2026-08-27T09:00:00Z",
  "currency": "USD",
  "total_cost": 12.418264,
  "groups": [
    {
      "key": "claude-sonnet-4",
      "count": 1841,
      "error_count": 12,
      "total_cost": 9.612055,
      "input_tokens": 2903118,
      "output_tokens": 214477,
      "latency": { "mean": 1840.2, "max": 24193.0 }
    },
    {
      "key": "gpt-4o-mini",
      "count": 6204,
      "error_count": 3,
      "total_cost": 2.806209,
      "input_tokens": 9114882,
      "output_tokens": 388310,
      "latency": { "mean": 612.4, "max": 8801.0 }
    }
  ]
}`,
  },
]
---

<Doc
  title="Cost accounting"
  description="How mcpd turns token counts into a cost figure: priced at ingest so history cannot be rewritten, cached tokens charged apart, and unknown models left unpriced rather than free."
  lede="A cost figure is only worth having if you can say where it came from. This is every step between a token count and a number on the screen."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>Priced once, at ingest</h2>
  <p>
    When a span arrives carrying a model id and a token count, mcpd looks that
    model up in a price book, computes what the call cost and stores the result
    on the span. Nothing is recomputed on read.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the important decision on this page. Prices change. If cost were
    derived at query time from whatever book happened to be loaded that day, a
    rate cut next month would quietly rewrite what last month cost, and the
    number you reported to somebody would not be there when they went to check
    it. Costing at ingest freezes the figure against the rates that were true
    when the call was made. Every stored cost also records the id of the book
    that produced it, so a figure can be traced back to the rates behind it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A span that already carries a cost is left exactly as it arrived. If your
    provider hands you a real figure and you record it, mcpd keeps yours and does
    not price over it. The <code>is_estimated</code> flag separates the two:
    anything mcpd derived from a book is marked estimated, because it is.
  </p>

  <h2>Rates are per million tokens</h2>
  <p>
    Every rate in a price book is currency units per million tokens, which is how
    every provider publishes them. A rate copied off a pricing page goes into a
    book unchanged, with no division to get wrong at three in the morning. The
    arithmetic for one meter is
    <code>tokens × rate ÷ 1,000,000</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Cost lives on the step, not on the run</h2>
  <p>
    Each model call carries its own figure. A run's total is the sum of its
    spans, and mcpd recomputes that sum from the spans every time one of them
    arrives rather than incrementing a counter. Spans arrive out of order, get
    retried and get updated in place, and an incrementing total drifts under all
    three. A headline number that does not match the rows underneath it destroys
    trust in every other number on the page.
  </p>

  <Waterfall class="not-prose my-8" />

  <p>
    The three model calls above are priced at $0.00015, $0.00018 and $0.00021.
    The run total in the header is their sum and nothing else: the tool calls,
    the memory lookup and the guardrail consumed no tokens, so they carry no cost
    and contribute none.
  </p>

  <h2>The meters</h2>
  <p>
    A model call is not one number of tokens. Providers bill several meters at
    different rates, and a price book gives a rate for each of them.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Rate</th><th>Applies to</th><th>When the book omits it</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>input</code></td>
        <td>Ordinary input tokens.</td>
        <td>Required.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>output</code></td>
        <td>Ordinary completion tokens.</td>
        <td>Required.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>cache_read</code></td>
        <td>Tokens served from a prompt cache.</td>
        <td>Charged at <code>input</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>cache_write</code></td>
        <td>Tokens written into a prompt cache.</td>
        <td>Charged at <code>input</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>cache_write_5m</code></td>
        <td>Writes to a five-minute cache, where the provider prices TTLs apart.</td>
        <td>Falls back to <code>cache_write</code>, then <code>input</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>cache_write_1h</code></td>
        <td>Writes to a one-hour cache.</td>
        <td>Falls back to <code>cache_write</code>, then <code>input</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>audio_input</code></td>
        <td>Audio input tokens.</td>
        <td>Charged at <code>input</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>audio_output</code></td>
        <td>Audio output tokens.</td>
        <td>Charged at <code>output</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>reasoning</code></td>
        <td>Hidden reasoning tokens, where a provider bills them apart.</td>
        <td>
          No fallback. They are charged at <code>output</code> as part of the
          output total, which is how the major providers bill them, unless the
          provider also reported a separate <code>text</code> count. See below.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>A cached token is charged once</h2>
  <p>
    The classic way to overstate a bill is to charge a cached token twice: once
    at the cache rate, and again as part of the reported input total it is
    already inside. So when a provider reports a breakdown, that breakdown wins.
    mcpd prices each meter at its own rate and charges only the remainder at the
    plain input rate.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <code>claude-sonnet-4</code> call reporting one million input tokens, of
    which nine hundred thousand were read from cache, prices against the built-in
    book like this:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>900,000 cache reads at $0.30 per million, which is $0.27.</li>
    <li>100,000 remaining input tokens at $3.00 per million, which is $0.30.</li>
    <li>
      $0.57 of input cost, rather than the $3.00 the reported total on its own
      would have produced.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Where a provider gives an explicit <code>text</code> count, that count is
    used as the remainder directly. Where it does not, the remainder is the
    reported total minus every metered field.
  </p>
  <p>
    One rule covers meters without a rate. A meter the book holds no rate for is
    charged only as part of the remainder, so when the provider also sent a
    <code>text</code> count it is not charged at all. Reasoning tokens sit there
    whenever your book gives them no rate of their own, and declaring one fixes
    it. Image tokens sit there always, because a book has no image rate to give,
    so on a heavily multimodal workload read the figure as low rather than exact.
  </p>

  <h2>An unknown model has no cost, not a cost of zero</h2>
  <p>
    If the book holds no rates for the model, mcpd stores the span with no cost
    field at all. It does not store a zero.
  </p>
  <p>
    Zero is a claim: this call was free. Absent says the book
    holds no price for this call. They lead to different decisions, and a tool that
    reports the second as the first is lying about a bill. The same rule runs
    through the read path, where a run whose spans total nothing is returned with
    no cost figure rather than a cost of zero, so a blank cost column means
    <em>not priced</em> and never <em>free</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The practical consequence is worth internalising: a model missing from the
    book is missing from every total. Its spend does not appear in
    <code>/v1/api/analytics/cost</code>, and its runs are excluded by any
    positive <code>min_cost</code> filter. A weekly total that looks too low is
    usually a model id that is not resolving, not a quiet week.
  </p>

  <h2>Resolving a model id</h2>
  <p>
    The id on a span is whatever your client sent, and clients decorate it. A
    gateway prefixes its vendor, a cloud prefixes its region, a provider pins a
    release date. A book is keyed on bare model names, so lookup tries four
    things in order and stops at the first hit.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Step</th><th>Example</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>The id as given, case folded</td>
        <td><code>GPT-4o</code> finds <code>gpt-4o</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The part after the last slash</td>
        <td><code>openai/gpt-4o</code> finds <code>gpt-4o</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The dotted tail of a cloud id</td>
        <td>
          <code>us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4</code> finds
          <code>claude-sonnet-4</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The id with a trailing date removed, with or without its prefix</td>
        <td>
          <code>openai/gpt-4o-2024-08-06</code> finds <code>gpt-4o</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Both <code>-YYYY-MM-DD</code> and <code>-YYYYMMDD</code> suffixes are
    recognised. Failing all four steps is a normal outcome rather than an error:
    it means no cost, as above.
  </p>

  <h2>The book that ships</h2>
  <p>
    mcpd carries a small built-in book so that <code>docker run</code> produces
    real figures with no configuration at all. It is called
    <code>builtin</code>, it prices in USD, and every cost it produces records
    that name.
  </p>

  {/* Transcribed from `builtin_book` in crates/mcpd-core/src/cost.rs. When a
      rate moves there, it moves here. */}
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Model</th><th>Input</th><th>Output</th><th>Cache read</th><th>Cache write, 5 min</th><th>Cache write, 1 hour</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>claude-opus-4</code></td><td>15.00</td><td>75.00</td><td>1.50</td><td>18.75</td><td>30.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>claude-opus-4-1</code></td><td>15.00</td><td>75.00</td><td>1.50</td><td>18.75</td><td>30.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>claude-opus-4-5</code></td><td>5.00</td><td>25.00</td><td>0.50</td><td>6.25</td><td>10.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>claude-sonnet-4</code></td><td>3.00</td><td>15.00</td><td>0.30</td><td>3.75</td><td>6.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>claude-sonnet-4-5</code></td><td>3.00</td><td>15.00</td><td>0.30</td><td>3.75</td><td>6.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>claude-haiku-4-5</code></td><td>1.00</td><td>5.00</td><td>0.10</td><td>1.25</td><td>2.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>claude-3-5-haiku</code></td><td>0.80</td><td>4.00</td><td>0.08</td><td>1.00</td><td>1.60</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>gpt-4o</code></td><td>2.50</td><td>10.00</td><td>1.25</td><td>at input</td><td>at input</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>gpt-4o-mini</code></td><td>0.15</td><td>0.60</td><td>0.075</td><td>at input</td><td>at input</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>gpt-4.1</code></td><td>2.00</td><td>8.00</td><td>0.50</td><td>at input</td><td>at input</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>gpt-4.1-mini</code></td><td>0.40</td><td>1.60</td><td>0.10</td><td>at input</td><td>at input</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>o3</code></td><td>2.00</td><td>8.00</td><td>0.50</td><td>at input</td><td>at input</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>o4-mini</code></td><td>1.10</td><td>4.40</td><td>0.275</td><td>at input</td><td>at input</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>gemini-2.5-pro</code></td><td>1.25</td><td>10.00</td><td>0.31</td><td>at input</td><td>at input</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>gemini-2.5-flash</code></td><td>0.30</td><td>2.50</td><td>0.075</td><td>at input</td><td>at input</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>text-embedding-3-small</code></td><td>0.02</td><td>0.00</td><td>at input</td><td>at input</td><td>at input</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>text-embedding-3-large</code></td><td>0.13</td><td>0.00</td><td>at input</td><td>at input</td><td>at input</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    USD per million tokens, as the book stood when it was last checked against
    a third-party model catalogue, which the book records as its
    <code>as_of</code> date. Anthropic's entries carry both cache write rates
    because the daemon falls back from the one-hour rate to the five-minute one,
    and a book that omitted the longer one would under-bill in silence. OpenAI
    charges nothing to write to its cache, so a cache write there is billed at
    the input rate. <code>gemini-2.5-flash</code> also carries an audio input
    rate of 1.00; every other entry prices audio at its text rate. There is no
    <code>claude-haiku-4</code>: the id Anthropic serves is
    <code>claude-haiku-4-5</code>, and an id that resolves to nothing is left
    unpriced rather than guessed.
  </p>
  <p>
    These are published list prices in US dollars. To price with your own
    rates, such as negotiated prices or a model the table does not name, pass a
    book with <code>--price-book rates.json</code> (or
    <code>MCPD_PRICE_BOOK</code>). Its entries layer over the built-in table: a
    model it names takes its rate, and every other model keeps the built-in
    one. A book in another currency replaces the built-in table.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`{
  "id": "acme-2026-10",
  "currency": "USD",
  "models": {
    "gpt-4.1": { "input": 1.60, "output": 6.40 }
  },
  "providers": {
    "azure": { "gpt-4.1": { "input": 1.80, "output": 7.20 } }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    A book is an id, an ISO 4217 currency and a map of model id to rates. Every
    figure is stamped with the id of the book that produced it, so two books can
    be told apart after the fact, and the rates in one project share one
    currency: mcpd sums a run's spans arithmetically and converts between none
    of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the money back</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GET /v1/api/analytics/cost</code> answers where the money went over a
    window, without exporting anything to a spreadsheet.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    command="curl -s 'http://localhost:4317/v1/api/analytics/cost?from=-7d&group_by=model'"
  />

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>from</code>, <code>to</code></td>
        <td>
          The window. RFC 3339 or the relative shorthand. Defaults to the last
          seven days, ending now.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>group_by</code></td>
        <td>
          <code>model</code> by default. Also <code>provider</code>,
          <code>entity_name</code>, <code>environment</code>,
          <code>status</code>, <code>session_id</code>, <code>user_id</code>,
          <code>service_name</code> or <code>name</code>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>bucket</code></td>
        <td>
          <code>30s</code>, <code>5m</code>, <code>1h</code> or <code>1d</code>.
          Omit it for totals only; supply it to get a time series alongside them.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>project</code></td>
        <td>Which project to read. Defaults to the daemon's default project.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" samples={breakdown} title="where it went" />

  <p>
    Groups come back largest first, capped at a hundred rows, and a bucketed
    series is capped at five thousand points.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" samples={response} title="response" />

  <p>
    One subtlety in <code>count</code>. Grouping by <code>model</code>,
    <code>provider</code> or <code>entity_name</code> reads the spans, so
    <code>count</code> is a number of model calls. Grouping by anything else
    reads the run projection, so <code>count</code> is a number of runs. Both are
    useful; they are not the same number, and comparing one against the other is
    the easiest mistake to make here.
  </p>
  <p>
    The per-group latency in this response carries a mean and a maximum only.
    Percentiles come from
    <code>GET /v1/api/analytics/summary</code>, which computes them over the
    window.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <p>
    Cost is one column in a run list, and the run list is where an investigation
    actually starts:
    <a href="/docs/searching" class="link">Finding a run</a> covers
    <code>min_cost</code> and sorting by spend. If your figures look wrong
    because payloads are being dropped before they are priced, check
    <a href="/docs/sampling" class="link">Sampling</a>, which decides what is
    recorded at all. Every field named on this page is listed in the
    <a href="/docs/reference/data-model" class="link">data model</a>.
  </p>
</Doc>
